WEBVTT
NOTE Cedwyn film, frame 005, en. Timings are estimates from the script until the clip is rendered.

1
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:18.615
<v the monk of Llandough>Bore da. Mind the mud by the cross. The masons cut it from pale stone fetched from Sutton on the coast, and set it here by Dochdwy's church, where every soul going in to pray must pass it.

2
00:00:18.965 --> 00:00:27.427
<v The Grandson>It still stands. A pillar-cross of Sutton stone, carved some time in the tenth or eleventh century. Nobody can pin the year.

3
00:00:27.777 --> 00:00:37.392
<v the monk of Llandough>The letters on the shaft read IRBICI. I trace them for the boys learning their letters. Whose name it keeps, the stone does not tell.

4
00:00:37.742 --> 00:00:51.973
<v the monk of Llandough>Now word comes from Cardiff. The Norman lord Fitzhamon has given lands, this hill among them, to the abbey at Tewkesbury, and the prior of St Mary's in Cardiff is to be our lord of the manor.

5
00:00:52.323 --> 00:01:01.169
<v The Grandson>That 1106 grant reaches us through one page: a letter printed in the Penarth Times in 2011, on the history of St Mary's.

6
00:01:01.519 --> 00:01:13.442
<v The Grandson>A letter to a newspaper isn't the charter. It names Fitzhamon, Tewkesbury and the prior, but we haven't seen the grant itself, and it doesn't show which fields went with it.

7
00:01:13.792 --> 00:01:18.792
<v the monk of Llandough>Abbey or prior, the hay still wants cutting. Lords change; the hill stays.

8
00:01:19.142 --> 00:01:26.065
<v the monk of Llandough>Every spring we walk the bounds, stone to stone, cwm to cwm, and a clerk writes them down.

9
00:01:26.415 --> 00:01:37.569
<v The Grandson>And a clerk in 1392 may have done just that. The Grandson's folders hold a family research note pointing to Cwm-Cedwyn in the Llandough boundary records of that year.

10
00:01:37.919 --> 00:01:52.919
<v The Grandson>But plainly: we don't hold that record. Nobody here has seen the original, so the note doesn't show the 1392 record exists or what it holds, and it isn't proof of a Cedwyn Estate. It's a name to chase.

11
00:01:53.269 --> 00:02:02.500
<v The Grandson>The cross entry comes from a local-history blog, sconzani, on the Irbic cross. A good modern write-up; I'd still like the survey behind it.

12
00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:06.312
<v the monk of Llandough>Write it down, whoever you are. Stone outlasts parchment.

13
00:02:06.662 --> 00:02:09.354
<v The Grandson>Next, the leases. Paper we can hold.
